;;; An activator that binds a value parameter to a nested fact leaves the
;;; parameter unbound — regression `activator-nested-value`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Three `p` facts, one of them over the nested fact `(f a)`, a rule `act`
;;; whose parameter `?y` is an argument of its premise and its conclusion, and
;;; its activator `(act (f a))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An activator binds a rule's parameters to its arguments, and a nested fact
;;; is a value as any other: `?y` is `(f a)`, so `act` matches `(p (f a))`
;;; alone and root holds `(hit (f a))` and no other `hit`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The compiler skipped a nested fact when it seeded a rule's parameters (*a
;;; nested Fact does not bind*), so `?y` was left free and `act` fired on every
;;; `p`: root held `(hit a)`, `(hit b)` and `(hit (f a))`, H was empty and R
;;; {∅}, exit 0 (`ein saturate FILE --dump`, `ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle
;;; FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).
;;; The loader refused the same activator only where the parameter names a relation.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

;; An activator binds a rule's parameter, used as an argument, to a nested fact.
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation hit T :why "{?1} is a hit")
(p a) (p b) (p (f a))
(rule act (?y) :match (p ?y) :assert (hit ?y) :why "act is activated for {?y} and p holds of it, so {?y} is a hit")
(act (f a))
(query :goal (hit ?x))
